-## Cache Compatibility
-
-- SQLite is disposable derived state under `node_modules/.cache/encephalon`.
-- The repository, cache ancestors, SQLite databases and sidecars, operation-lock metadata, recovery entries, and quarantine entries must be real contained filesystem entries verified by type, native realpath, and stable identity. Mutable SQLite databases and sidecars must have exactly one hard link at every verification and quarantine boundary; canonical record publication retains its separate guarded hard-link contract. Static symlinks, junction redirects, hard-linked mutable SQLite files, unexpected types, and replacements at validation boundaries fail closed.
-- Missing cache ancestors are created individually. New primary databases use exclusive no-follow descriptor creation before SQLite opens the verified pathname, and destructive recovery removes only the exact identity moved to a verified sibling quarantine.
-- Operation-lock recovery retries only bounded sharing violations while exact directory identity and owner token remain current. Optional SQLite sidecar canonicalisation is likewise reobserved within the existing bound so normal WAL teardown cannot be confused with a persistent unsafe path.
-- After a gate transaction begins, SQLite close is permitted only while the captured cache containment remains current, the captured primary remains current and single linked, and bounded metadata-only observation proves every WAL, SHM, and journal pathname is either a stable single-link regular file or absent. Containment failures, redirects, observation errors, changed or unlinked generations, realpath mismatches, and multiply-linked files retain the connection because close-time journal cleanup can target the current pathname; the authoritative public error remains unchanged. A small fixed-capacity process-lifetime latch rejects another SQLite open for that database and rejects all further SQLite opens once the capacity is reached, bounding retained connections. Recovery requires correction of the unsafe cache layout and a process restart.
-- Every verified operation-gate acquisition, including corrupt-gate recovery, is serialised by a bounded owner marker and one total deadline. A corrupt or not-a-database result is confirmed by reopening the exact captured gate identity; only a second recoverable result authorises exact quarantine and exclusive successor creation. New recovery markers contain immutable canonical `recovering` owner metadata. After verified SQLite `BEGIN` succeeds while that exact directory, owner-file identity, bytes, and token remain current, the owner exclusively publishes a bounded, flushed `owner.recovered.json` witness with matching fields and `recovered` phase. Phase-less legacy owners remain `recovering`. An exact owner-plus-witness pair is reclaimable by any process regardless of PID liveness; a valid live recovering owner is never reclaimed because of age. Partial, malformed, hard-linked, oversized, mismatched, replaced, dead, or ownerless evidence remains fail-closed under its established stale-owner policy and is revalidated after quarantine by exact identity and metadata, plus exact raw bytes whenever bounded contents were captured. The SQLite transaction remains authoritative throughout. Cleanup failure prevents entry into the protected operation but cannot strand another process after recovered evidence is visible. Gate or publication failure remains primary over marker cleanup failure; gate cleanup reports its own failure only when the protected operation succeeded. Same-repository nested acquisition is unsupported; internal callers that already hold the operation lock propagate that held-lock state instead of reacquiring it.
-- Recovery-marker exclusion begins with atomic directory creation. An owner file that is briefly absent is age-reclaimed rather than published by candidate-directory rename because Node has no cross-platform no-replace directory rename, and replacement semantics could displace an empty live marker.
